Huntington disease is a degenerative  disease of the central nervous system  it's an inherited disease caused by a  mutation in the huntington gene symptoms  of Huntington disease typically develop  in adulthood and include changes in  movement cognition and behavior there is  no cure current treatments only reduce  the symptoms the normal function of the  Huntington gene is not fully understood  but it is important for the development  of the nervous system the mutation  itself is quite distinctive there are  too many repeats of a sequence in the  gene where the basis bel CAG this  trinucleotide codes for the amino acid  glutamine more than 40 repeats lead to a  mutated protein with an unusually long  chain of glutamine such a long protein  invariably causes disease mutated  Huntington proteins are broken down to  shorter fragments by caspases cal pains  and other proteases but how these  fragments cause toxicity is not wholly  clear the most studied fragment called h  TTX on one can take different  confirmations and combine to various  other proteins so it's a challenge to  find out how this fragment contributes  to disease development however h TTX on  one can form aggregates including  tangles of amyloid fibrils similar  aggregates are also present in other  neurodegenerative diseases including  outs Hyman disease Parkinson disease and  mi atrophic lateral sclerosis or ALS in  Huntington disease - these aggregates  might be responsible for the toxic  effects of mutated Huntington  you
 

